# Each phrase vouches for a fact the sentence already stated. The
# deliberate rule holds the phrases that vouch for a choice instead.
#
# Each phrase is pinned to its reassuring sense, because docs reuse
# the same words with a plain meaning. `no magic` skips a following
# noun (`no magic number`, `no magic bytes`). `no surprises` counts
# only at the end of a clause (`reported no surprises after the
# migration` is a report). `the whole point` needs `of`, `is`, `was`,
# or `that` after it, or `is` before it (`rounds to the whole point`
# and `the whole point cloud` are measurements). `no more and no less`
# skips a following `than`, which turns it into a bound, and so does
# its comma form (`no more, no less`). `nothing more, nothing less`
# never takes `than`, so it counts as written.
#
# `without changing it` stays unconditional. A contrast that matters
# (`Fsck checks the filesystem without changing it when invoked with
# -n`) reads better with the contrast named (`With -n, fsck only
# reports`), and the skill lists the phrase as the writer weighing a
# fact on the reader's behalf. `and nothing else` counts only at the
# end of a clause, because before a verb `nothing else` is the subject
# of a new clause (`touches the file and nothing else references it`).
# Its guard `else!( )` takes any token but whitespace right after the
# phrase, so it also counts at the end of a list item with no period,
# while a line break inside a paragraph reads as whitespace. A twin
# branch takes a space and then a token outside every part-of-speech
# tag with no space after it, which is how a pattern names the open
# paren of a citation (`and nothing else (see the keyword table)`),
# since Weir has no escape for a literal paren. The glued token keeps
# out a verb the tagger left untagged (`and nothing else deletes it`),
# because a space follows a word.
#
# The clause-end list is written glued to the word before it
# (`surprises[., ;, :, (,)]`), since a space in a pattern is a
# whitespace token and clause punctuation follows the word directly. It
# is spelled out each time because `surprises@end` tokenizes as one
# word. A bare comma cannot sit in a list, because the comma separates
# list items, so it is wrapped as a one-token sequence. A negated word
# after a space (`less !than`) needs a word to consume, so each of
# those branches has a clause-end twin.
expr tagged [ADJ, ADP, ADV, AUX, CCONJ, DET, INTJ, NOUN, NUM, PART, PRON, PROPN, SCONJ, SYM, VERB, X]
expr magic [number, numbers, byte, bytes, prefix, string, strings, value, values, constant, constants]
expr main [(no more and no less !than), (no more and no less[., ;, :, (,)]), (no more, no less !than), (no more, no less[., ;, :, (,)]), (nothing more, nothing less), (nothing more and nothing less), (and nothing else!( )), (and nothing else !@tagged!( )), (exactly what you would expect), (exactly what you'd expect), (no magic !@magic), (no magic[., ;, :, (,)]), (no surprises[., ;, :, (,)]), (as the name [suggests, implies]), (the [whole, entire] point [of, is, was, that]), ([is, was] the [whole, entire] point), (it does exactly that), (without changing it)]
let message "Reassurance. A stated fact needs no vouching, so delete the phrase."
let description "Flags phrases that insist on a fact the sentence already stated."
let becomes ""
let kind "Style"
let scope "Sentence"
allows "Flush writes the buffer to disk."
allows "Close releases the file handle."
allows "The file has no magic bytes, so the loader inspects the extension."
allows "There is no magic-number check."
allows "Users reported no surprises after the migration."
allows "The no surprises policy applies to all releases."
allows "The interest rate rounds to the whole point."
allows "The entire point cloud fits in memory."
allows "The reader reads no more and no less than n bytes, or returns an error."
allows "The reader reads no more, no less than n bytes."
allows "Probe touches the file and nothing else references it."
allows "The prune does not touch it, and nothing else deletes it."
